{"records":[{"id":"3ctpx4k6jp","title":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus upregulates SMPDL3B to promote viral replication by modulating lipid metabolism","description":"We generated a collection of porcine genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout (PigGeCKO) cells on a PAM background.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-26T18:53:54.241Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3ctpx4k6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ctpx4k6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huanh","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bdxc2v4xkm","title":"Photoactivatible HDL and its transport in experimental psoriasis","description":"Lipoproteins trapped in arteries drive atherosclerosis. Extravascular LDL ligates LDL receptor for cellular uptake, whereas HDL interacts with cells to pick up cellular cholesterol, and then recirculates to plasma. We developed photoactivatable apoA-I to understand how HDL passage through tissue is regulated. We focused on skin and arteries of healthy mice versus those with psoriasis, which carries cardiovascular risk in man. Our findings suggest that immunity, programmed in lymph nodes draining inflamed skin, allows for IL-17-producing T cells to home to distal skin and later to arteries, where little pathology is observed except remodeling of collagenous matrix, such that larger molecules generally become entrapped. HDL transit was rescued by depleting CD4+ T cells, neutralizing IL-17, or inhibiting lysyl oxidase that crosslinks collagen. Experimental psoriasis was systemically associated with vascular stiffness and increased atherosclerosis, also driven by IL-17. Thus, IL-17 can reduce lipoprotein trafficking by, at least in part, remodeling collagen.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-06T01:48:17.198Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bdxc2v4xkm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bdxc2v4xkm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gwendalyn","last_name":"Randolph","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Collagen","Fluorescence","Western Blot","Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"55r4vwgr2n","title":"Cellular uptake","description":"Cellular uptake experiment MNPs- Caco-2, HT-29, HCT-116, TR-146, HaCat","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-15T21:31:31.402Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/55r4vwgr2n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/55r4vwgr2n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yamm","last_name":"Anker","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cellular Process","Uptake Rates"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fm4h3mphcr","title":"NGS, confocal microscopy and flow cytometry data of CRISPR/Cas9-edited ARE1 and KAT2 genes in transgenic maize in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers","description":"This dataset contains raw NGS reads (NGS_data folder), fluorescence microscopy photos (Fig7B Microscopy Originals.lif) and flow cytometry data (@Fig6 FACS original data.zip) derived from transgenic maize (Zea mays L.) in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers. They are presented in research article ˝Variable Alterations in Frequencies of CRISPR/Cas9-Induced Nucleotide Deletion and Insertion in Transgenic Maize Callus Tissues Exposed to Chromatin Modifiers˝.\nStable transgenic maize lines harboring active CRISPR/Cas9 vector systems targeting either the abnormal cytokinin response 1 (ARE1) gene or the potassium channel gene (KAT2) were used. Mutagenic events following exposure to elevated temperatures (37 °C for 48 h) and various chromatin-modifying compounds were quantified. As a novel approach, the mutagenic potential of combining chromatin-modifying agents with heat exposure was examined.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:17:12.229Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fm4h3mphcr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fm4h3mphcr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bettina","last_name":"Nagy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Györgyi","last_name":"Ferenc","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ferhan","last_name":"Ayaydin","orcid":"0000-0003-4450-0667"},{"first_name":"Divya","last_name":"Teja","orcid":"0009-0001-0210-7878"},{"first_name":"Dénes","last_name":"Dudits","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zoltán","last_name":"Zombori","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikolett","last_name":"Laszlo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aysha","last_name":"Jameel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gábor","last_name":"Horváth","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"EU’s Horizon 2020 Research and Innovation Program","grant_id":" 739593"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03g2am276","name":"National Research, Development and Innovation Office","grant_id":"RRF 2.3.1–21-2022- 00007 grant for the ‘National Laboratory Program of Agrobiotechnology and Precision Plant Breeding to Support Food Safety’"}],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Flow Cytometry","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5z7ppzwpcw","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 screening of the deubiquitinase subfamily identifies USP49 as a protein stabilizer of PAX9 and MSX1 regulating odontogenesis","description":"Deubiquitination of proteins by DUBs has emerged as a key regulator of protein stability and has been shown to be critically involved in signal transduction cascades. However, the function and regulation of individual DUBs are not well understood at the molecular level. Several screening approaches to identify DUBs and their target protein substrates in diverse cellular functions have been reported previously. For example, shRNA/siRNA libraries targeting different DUBs or overexpression of ectopically introduced DUBs have been used to investigate the impact of DUB function on target substrate protein stability. These conventional methods have several limitations, such as the limitation of RNAi or shRNA-based methods to transcripts, and these techniques often result in partial repression or restoration of gene functions in a few generations, with temporary effects.  The results obtained from these methods may not be accurate and might lead to misinterpretations of the data. To overcome this, we developed a loss-of-function study based on individually generated single cell-derived biallelic DUB knockout clones in mammalian cells and provide an alternate and improved model to explore the molecular mechanism of DUBs.\n\nIn this study, we used CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated) mediated DUB knockout screening kit based on genome-scale knockout of the entire set of genes that encode human ubiquitin-specific proteases (USPs), which are the largest subfamily of DUBs (>50 genes), and then we systematically applied the panel to screen DUB candidates that might regulate PAX9 and MSX1 proteins. PAX9 and MSX1 are critical for the transition in odontogenic potential from epithelium to mesenchyme. Although genetic mutations in PAX9 and MSX1 cause tooth agenesis, the regulation of PAX9 and MSX1 protein levels by deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) might be a crucial factor during tooth development. Our screening system identified USP49 as a key regulator of PAX9 and MSX1 protein stability. USP49 interacts and deubiquitinates PAX9 and MSX1 protein and upregulates several odontogenic transcription factors in human dental pulp stem cells (hDPSCs). In animal models, USP49 depletion was correlated with downregulation of Pax9 and Msx1 protein expression resulting in several morphological defects in calcified tissues such as reduced dentin growth, reduced enamel space, abnormal enamel formation, and irregular mineralization. Thus, the depletion of USP49 leads to an inadequate transition from bud to cap stage in the early stages of tooth development. This novel finding suggests that the USP49-PAX9-MSX1 axis could serve as a prognostic marker and novel regulatory target in odontogenesis and can serve as a catalyst for bioengineering tooth implant research to improve clinical care.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-08T12:30:21.375Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5z7ppzwpcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5z7ppzwpcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suresh","last_name":"Ramakrishna","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Stem Cell","Animal Development","Tooth Enamel","Tooth Development"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":136739,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":155,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":136739,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":136092,"restricted":68,"embargoed":579},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2256},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1339},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":665},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":463},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":432},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":330},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":268}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":134107},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1902},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":180},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":867},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":789},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":422},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":359}]}}